artf media-screenshots-script : files = {
  path = "assets/capture/screenshots.mts"
  tags = ["type:capture"]

  eval records-scenes : luna = {
    title = "The script captures scenes A, B and C from the real app"
    red_at = "P1"
    instruction = """
      Read @{artf media-screenshots-script}, its shared capture lifecycle in @{artf media-capture-lifecycle}, the app's feature journeys in @{artf desktop-ux-features}, and the app in @{artf desktop-backend}, @{artf desktop-ui} and @{artf desktop-e2e} as far as the scenes need. Judge whether running the script captures each scene below from the real desktop app at 1920×1080, with real data made through the real CLI and nothing mocked:

      A. Verify (J-BAR, J-PANE, J-BRANCH-RUNS, J-MAIN-RUN, J-GRAPH-COLOURS): the Pane with the example's branch and its Run timeline, the Run's graph with mixed verdicts (GREEN, RED, blocked and waiting for a person), and the Review button's waiting count in the bottom bar.
      B. An Artifact's Detail (J-DETAIL, J-DETAIL-NEIGHBOURS) docked over the Run's graph on its Artifact tab: the Artifact between its neighbours, and its evals with their verdicts.
      C. Switch to Review from the bottom bar, select the waiting sign-off in Review's Pane and claim it (J-BAR, J-REVIEW-LIST, J-HUMAN-CLAIM). Show its conversation: the instruction and its tools, the finished notes tool's run line unfolded into its lines, an issue added from the issues box standing as a bubble, and the decision bar with RED chosen (J-HUMAN-LAYOUT, J-HUMAN-LOG, J-HUMAN-ISSUES, J-HUMAN-RED). Do not submit the review.

      Check that each scene's selectors and actions reach the state the journey describes, that the script waits for that state rather than a fixed delay alone before capturing, and that it cleans up the app, the build and the project it made. Check also that every scene is captured.
    """
  }
}
